content.text:
**Pushed** — `158760c77`, on top of the rebase. #1122 is ready (not draft), so the review when the slot opens will land on this head.

I resolved it by **removing** the second state path rather than adding a `finally` to it. `runBatch` now routes through `dispatchOrFail`, so `run`'s `try/finally` is the only busy/error handling in the hook, and the batch contributes only the per-node accounting:

```ts
const accepted: Uint8Array[] = [];
const ok = await dispatchOrFail(command, async (facade) => {
  const refusals: unknown[] = [];
  for (const node of nodes) {
    try { await dispatch(facade, node); accepted.push(node); }
    catch (failure: unknown) { refusals.push(failure); }
  }
  if (refusals.length > 0) throw refusals[0];
});
return { ok, accepted };
```

Net −7 lines. Three things fell out of it:

- The duplicated `client === null` guard is gone — `dispatchOrFail` already returns `false` with the not-ready error, which yields `{ ok: false, accepted: [] }` exactly as before.
- `NOT_READY` went back to an inline literal. It was extracted only because two call sites needed it; with one site left, the constant was pointless indirection. This restores that line to what CodeRabbit already reviewed at `bfc79dede`.
- The `throw refusals[0]` idiom is the one the original `perNode` used before the batch-outcome commit, so the error surface is unchanged.

Behaviour is identical in all three paths — engine not ready, partial refusal, and full success — including `errorMessage(refusals[0])` as the reported failure. `accepted` is safe to read after `await` because `run` fully awaits the closure before resolving.

Gates: `pnpm --filter @cipherbox/web test` 0 (28 files, 245 tests — same counts as before the change, so the existing batch tests cover it), `pnpm typecheck` 0, `pnpm lint` 0.

Worth noting the tests passing unchanged is weak evidence here — they'd pass against either version, since the divergence was latent rather than live. The value is that a future edit letting an exception escape can no longer strand `busy`.
